vscode: F2 rename, if the language server is awake
open: cancellation.ts

@brief
Eight times: `cancellationReason` becomes `cancelReason` everywhere in
the file. Yank the old name once and let `Ctrl-R 0` type it into the
substitute. You should never type the long name.

@keys
| `yiw` | yank the word under the cursor |
| `:%s/` `Ctrl-R 0` `/new/g` | the yank becomes the pattern |
| `Ctrl-R /` | the last search pattern, on the command line |

@hints
`2w` puts the cursor on the name in the first line. `yiw`, then start
the substitute and press `Ctrl-R 0` where the pattern goes.
--
`2w yiw :%s/` `Ctrl-R 0` `/cancelReason/g` Enter.

@solution
    2w yiw :%s/<Ctrl-R>0/cancelReason/g<Enter> :wq<Enter>

Eighteen characters of identifier, one chord. It is the same `Ctrl-R`
as insert mode, and the same registers: `Ctrl-R a` for a named one,
`Ctrl-R %` for the file name (`:!prettier --write` `Ctrl-R %` is a
common one), `Ctrl-R /` for the last search, so a pattern you have
already found with `/` becomes the pattern of a `:s` or `:g` without
retyping.

Chapter 12 gave you `*`, which searches for the word under the cursor
with word boundaries, and chapter 14 gave you the empty pattern in
`:%s//new/g`, which reuses the last search. `*` then `:%s//cancelReason/g`
is a fair rival here. `Ctrl-R 0` wins when the thing you want is not
under the cursor any more.

@start cancellation.ts
export const cancellationReason = 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED';

export function cancel(order: Order) {
  if (order.cancellationReason) {
    return order;
  }

  return { ...order, status: 'CANCELLED', cancellationReason };
}

export function wasRefunded(order: Order) {
  return order.cancellationReason !== cancellationReason;
}

@expected cancellation.ts
export const cancelReason = 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED';

export function cancel(order: Order) {
  if (order.cancelReason) {
    return order;
  }

  return { ...order, status: 'CANCELLED', cancelReason };
}

export function wasRefunded(order: Order) {
  return order.cancelReason !== cancelReason;
}
